      "math_academy_id": "q-11809",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096038/assets/8a48fcacc437d1dca41df82e24984507a92d686f80f4517c6682e8b14b694f0e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-6,0)$ and passes through the point $(-2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,0),$ we substitute $h=-6$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-2,6),$ so we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6) \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(-2+6) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})(x+6) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{9}{4}$ is positive.",
