      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,-3),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (2+3)^{2} & =4p(1+4) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =5(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
